erminology as long as there is no chance for confusion.\n\nRemark 1 Let us explain some intuition for these conditions. (A) is self-explanatory; in (B), one may keep in mind the examples Image $g(d) = 1/d$ or Image $g(d) = 1/\\phi (d)$ ; and (C) may be thought of as a more technical condition – the reader may check that it is trivially satisfied if the sequence Image $(a_n)$ is bounded and Image $N(x) \\gg x/(\\log x)^B$ for some B.\n\nExamples of sequences satisfying these conditions are described by the following propositions. Throughout the article, for a proposition Image $\\mathfrak {A}$ , we use the notation Image $\\mathbf {1}[\\mathfrak {A}]$ to be Image $1$ if Image $\\mathfrak {A}$ is true and Image $0$ otherwise.\n\nProposition 2 Shifted primes are Image $1/2$ well-distributed. That is, for a fixed integer a, consider the set Image $\\mathcal {B} = \\{p-a:\\, p\\; \\textrm {is prime}\\}$ and let Image $a_n = \\mathbf {1}[n \\in \\mathcal {B}]$ . Then Image $(a_n)$ has index Image $1$ , has level of distribution Image $\\vartheta = 1/2$ , and is congruence uniform.\n\nProof That the sequence has index Image $\\alpha = 1$ follows from the prime number theorem (or indeed Chebyshev’s bounds). By Remark 1, congruence uniformity also follows from Chebyshev’s lower bound Image $\\pi (x) \\gg x/\\log x$ . The level of distribution Image $1/2$ follows from the Bombieri–Vinogradov theorem [Reference Cojocaru and Murty6, Theorem 9.2.1], with Image $g(d) = \\mathbf {1}_{(a,d)=1}/\\phi (d)$ , where Image $\\mathbf {1}_{(a,d)=1}$ is Image $1$ when d is coprime to a and Image $0$ otherwise.\n\nProposition 3 The values of irreducible polynomials of degree D are Image $1/D$ well-distributed. That is, for a polynomial Image $F(X) \\in \\mathbb {Z}[X]$ of degree Image $D\\geq 1,$ which is irreducible with positive leading coefficient, consider the set Image $\\mathcal {C} = \\{ F(n):\\, n \\in \\mathbb {N}_{\\geq 1} \\} \\cap \\mathbb {N}_{\\geq 1}$ and let Image $a_n = \\mathbf {1}[n \\in \\mathcal {C}]$ . Then Image $(a_n)$ has index Image $1/D$ , has level of distribution Image $\\vartheta = 1/D$ , and is congruence uniform.\n\n### 1.3 Main results\n\nOur main results depend on the following setup. As above, we let Image $(a_n)$ be a sequence of nonnegative numbers, and for a parameter Image $x,$ we let u be a random integer such that\n\nImage $$ \\begin{align*}\\mathbb{P}(u = m) = \\frac{a_m}{N(x)} \\mathbf{1}[1 \\leq m \\leq x]. \\end{align*} $$\n\nIn the case that Image $a_n$ is the indicator function of a subset of natural numbers, u is uniformly distributed on elements of the set no more than x. As before, we let Image $p_1 \\geq p_2 \\geq \\cdots $ be the prime factors of u listed with multiplicity, with the convention that Image $p_j = 1$ if n has fewer than j prime factors.\n\nWe will prove results comparing the distribution of Image $p_1,p_2,\\ldots $ to a Poisson–Dirichlet process (Theorem 7 and Lemma 8) and also an upper bound for the likelihood that Image $p_1 = P^+(u)$ is exceptionally large (Theorem 11). These results are related in that they use almost the same information, but because they may be of independent interest, we have written this note so that their proofs may be read independently.\n\nTheorem 7 If Image $(a_n)$ is Image $1$ well-distributed, then\n\nImage $$ \\begin{align*}\\mathbb{P}\\Big(\\frac{\\log p_1}{\\log u} \\leq c_1,\\dots, \\frac{\\log p_k}{\\log u} \\leq c_k\\Big) \\rightarrow \\mathbb{P}(L_1 \\leq c_1,\\ldots,L_k \\leq c_k), \\end{align*} $$\n\nas Image $x\\rightarrow \\infty $ . That is, the process Image $\\tfrac {\\log p_1}{\\log u}, \\tfrac {\\log p_2}{\\log u},\\ldots $ tends in distribution to the Poisson–Dirichlet process Image $L_1, L_2,\\ldots $ .\n\nThis generalizes to multiple prime factors a result noted by Granville [Reference Granville22] (with details of the proof provided by Wang [Reference Wang42]) that for shifted primes the Elliott–Halberstam conjecture implies the distribution of the largest prime divisor is governed by the Dickman function (a phenomenon first conjectured by Pomerance [Reference Pomerance37]). And this result proves unconditionally that large prime factors of the Thue–Morse tend to a Poisson–Dirichlet distribution.\n\nOn the other hand, while the values of irreducible polynomials do not appear to have level of distribution Image $1$ , it is reasonable to believe that their prime factors tend to a Poisson–Dirichlet distribution. That the distribution of the largest prime factor is governed by the Dickman function was given a conditional proof by Martin [Reference Martin31] on the assumption of a prime number theorem for polynomial sequences. It may be possible to formulate a relaxed version of level of distribution Image $1$ which applies to the values of irreducible polynomials and which also implies a Poisson–Dirichlet distribution for large prime factors, but we do not pursue this further here. (Indeed the condition Image $(D_\\sigma )$ in the very recent preprint [Reference Mounier34] might be a correct starting point.)\n\nEven for a sequence with level of distribution less than 1, one may still compare the correlation functions of its large prime factors to those of the Poisson–Dirichlet process, at least against test functions with restricted support.\n\nLemma 8 If Image $(a_n)$ is Image $\\sigma $ well-distributed, then for any Image ${k \\geq 1}$ and any continuous Image ${\\eta : \\mathbb {R}^k \\rightarrow \\mathbb {C}}$ with Image $\\operatorname {\\mathrm {supp}} \\eta \\subset \\{y \\in \\mathbb {R}_+^k:\\, y_1 + \\cdots + y_k < \\sigma \\}$ (so, in particular, Image $\\eta $ is compactly supported),\n\nWe have adopted the convention for such a u that Image $p_j=1$ for Image $j\\geq 4$ , but because of the support of Image $\\eta $ such terms do not appear in this sum. Note that the sum is symmetric in Image $p_1, p_2$ , and Image $p_3$ .\n\nThe left-hand side of (6) will then be an average over u of sums of this type.\n\nLemma 8 has a surface-level resemblance to results that can be proven about zeros of L-functions. The reader unfamiliar with correlation sums as occur in the lemma may consult [Reference Hough, Krishnapur, Peres and Virág26, Chapter 1] for a general introduction and further information.\n\nLemma 8 gives information about prime divisors of intermediate size, but because of restrictions on the support of Image $\\eta ,$ it does not entail an asymptotic formula for the distribution of the largest prime factor of u. Our last result shows that even this partial information about the level of distribution entails an upper bound for how often the largest prime factor can be especially large.\n\nTheorem 11 If Image $(a_n)$ is Image $\\sigma $ well-distributed for some Image $\\sigma> 0$ , then for any Image $\\epsilon> 0$ ,\n\nImage $$ \\begin{align*}\\limsup_{x\\rightarrow\\infty}\\mathbb{P}(P^+(u) \\geq u^{1-\\epsilon}) \\ll \\epsilon, \\end{align*} $$\n\nwhere the implicit constant depends on the sequence Image $(a_n)$ .\n\nWe note that this is an essentially optimal result, since for sequences with level of distribution Image $1$ by Theorem 7, we have Image $\\mathbb {P}(P^+(u) \\geq u^{1-\\epsilon }) \\sim 1- \\rho (1/(1-\\epsilon ))$ , and for Image $\\epsilon $ small enough that Image $1\\leq 1/(1-\\epsilon ) \\leq 2,$ we have Image $1- \\rho (1/(1-\\epsilon )) = \\log (1/(1-\\epsilon )) \\approx \\epsilon $ (see [Reference Montgomery and Vaughan33, Equation (7.10)] for the evaluation of Image $\\rho $ in this range).\n\nIn the case of sampling shifted primes Image $p-1 \\leq x$ , Theorem 11 recovers the following corollary.\n\n## Acknowledgments\n\nWe thank Yuchen Ding, Ofir Gorodetsky, Ram Murty, and anonymous referees for comments, suggestions, and corrections to earlier versions of this manuscript. We have used ChatGPT 5.2 to proofread a version of the manuscript and to help format references but no parts of the article were computer generated.